Title: No Longer Poor: Ghana’s New Income Status and Implications of Graduation from IDA
Abstract: Ghana’s largest and most important creditor for the past three decades has been the International 
Development Association (IDA), the soft loan window of the World Bank. That will soon come 
to an end. The combination of Ghana’s rapid economic growth and the recent GDP rebasing 
exercise means that Ghana suddenly finds itself above the income limit for IDA eligibility.  Formal 
graduation is imminent and comes with significant implications for access to concessional finance, 
debt, and relations with other creditors.  This paper considers the specific questions related to 
Ghana’s relationship with the World Bank, as well as the broader questions about the country’s new 
middle-income status.
